{%- import "rust/_types.jinja" as types -%}
{#-
  The contract of one table's data layer: create, read, update, delete, and a
  search that filters over several columns and orders by a named column and a
  direction.

  Rendered once per table, per FR-RND-002 and FR-EX-005:

      tpl -d <schema> render rust/repository --table <name> > src/<schema>/<name>_repository.rs

  The trait is rendered separately from the implementation so that a caller can
  depend on the contract without depending on how it is served, and so that the
  difference between the two is visible in the example rather than implied.

  Every method returns `impl Future<Output = …> + Send` rather than being
  written `async fn`. The two desugar to the same thing, and the explicit form
  is what puts `Send` on the returned future, which an implementation is then
  obliged to honour and a caller may rely on when it spawns the call onto a
  work-stealing runtime.
-#}
{%- if not table.primary_key -%}
{{- fail("rust/repository.jinja cannot render " ~ database.name ~ "." ~ table.name ~ ": the table declares no primary key, so read, update and delete have no key to address a row by.") -}}
{%- endif -%}
{%- set model = types.type_name(table.name) -%}
{%- set module = types.ident(table.name) -%}
{%- set generated -%}
{%- for column in table.columns %}{% if column is auto_increment %}<auto>{% endif %}{% endfor -%}
{%- endset -%}
